1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,321
Before a program runs, it is loaded into the code segment of memory,

2
00:00:10,321 --> 00:00:16,433
where each instruction is a thirty-two bit word that takes four bytes, one right after another.

3
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:24,495
The processor is made of a control unit and a datapath, and the datapath holds the thirty-two registers, x0 to x31.

4
00:00:24,495 --> 00:00:30,594
The PC also lives in the datapath, and it holds the address of the current instruction.

5
00:00:30,594 --> 00:00:38,733
It is a register too, but it isn't one of x0 to x31, and instructions don't normally read or write it directly.

6
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:45,485
The control unit repeats four steps forever, which are reading the PC, fetching the instruction from memory,

7
00:00:45,485 --> 00:00:47,966
executing it, and updating the PC.

8
00:00:47,966 --> 00:00:52,195
Let's walk through an example from the course notes.

9
00:00:52,195 --> 00:00:56,831
In the first step we read the PC, which gives 0x00000008.

10
00:00:56,831 --> 00:01:06,103
In the second step we fetch the instruction at that address from memory, where it is just a thirty-two bit number,

11
00:01:06,103 --> 00:01:07,366
0x01051613.

12
00:01:07,366 --> 00:01:11,772
The control unit reads that number as slli x12 x10 0x10.

13
00:01:11,772 --> 00:01:17,593
This episode follows the notes in leaving out the commas between operands,

14
00:01:17,593 --> 00:01:21,800
and the encoding details wait until episode nine.

15
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,652
In step three we execute.

16
00:01:24,652 --> 00:01:38,266
The register x10 holds 0x000034FF, and shifting it left by 0x10 bits, which is sixteen, makes x12 become 0x34FF0000.

17
00:01:38,266 --> 00:01:40,712
In step four we update the PC.

18
00:01:40,712 --> 00:01:46,175
Every instruction takes four bytes, so by default the PC adds four,

19
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:50,822
becoming 0x0000000C and pointing at the next instruction.

20
00:01:50,822 --> 00:01:59,433
So this arithmetic instruction really changed two registers, the destination register x12 and the PC.

21
00:01:59,433 --> 00:02:04,229
Branches and jumps instead put the address of a label into the PC in step four,

22
00:02:04,229 --> 00:02:09,633
although a conditional branch does this only when its condition holds.

23
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:14,419
Those five words in memory are written in assembly as these four lines,

24
00:02:14,419 --> 00:02:18,127
and they are exactly an exercise from the course notes.

25
00:02:18,127 --> 00:02:24,465
The li instruction wants to load 0x34FF, which is beyond what a twelve-bit immediate can hold.

26
00:02:24,465 --> 00:02:32,100
So it expands into two instructions, which we'll cover in episode twelve, and that is why slli sits at 0x08.

27
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,430
Here is the question.

28
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:36,155
After these four lines run, what is in x12?

29
00:02:36,155 --> 00:02:42,066
Pause the video and think about it for yourself.

30
00:02:42,066 --> 00:02:46,625
After the first instruction runs, x10 is 0x000034FF.

31
00:02:46,625 --> 00:02:54,866
Let's draw all thirty-two bits, where every four bits correspond to one hexadecimal digit.

32
00:02:54,866 --> 00:03:03,134
The slli instruction shifts left by sixteen and fills zeros on the right, so x12 becomes 0x34FF0000,

33
00:03:03,134 --> 00:03:06,690
the same as the processor computed earlier.

34
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:14,545
The srli instruction then shifts right by eight and fills zeros on the left, giving 0x0034FF00,

35
00:03:14,545 --> 00:03:20,766
and since the top bit was zero anyway, srai would give the same answer.

36
00:03:20,766 --> 00:03:28,356
Finally, the and instruction works bit by bit, and gives a one only where both bits are one.

37
00:03:28,356 --> 00:03:36,633
Only three columns have a one in both rows, so x12 is 0x00003400, and the answer is B.

38
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:42,942
A branch compares and then jumps, but sometimes we want only the result of the comparison,

39
00:03:42,942 --> 00:03:47,498
such as the C statement that sets lt to whether a is less than b.

40
00:03:47,498 --> 00:03:50,092
It is one if true, and zero if false.

41
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:58,600
The matching instruction is slt, set less than, which sets rd to one if rs1 is less than rs2, and to zero otherwise.

42
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:07,128
Suppose a and b are in x5 and x6, with the result going into x7, so we write slt x7 x5 x6.

43
00:04:07,128 --> 00:04:11,866
Since three is less than five, x7 becomes one.

44
00:04:11,866 --> 00:04:17,262
The sltu instruction is the unsigned version, where the u stands for unsigned.

45
00:04:17,262 --> 00:04:19,961
When do the two give different results?

46
00:04:19,961 --> 00:04:24,457
Take x5 equal to 0xFFFFFFFF, with all thirty-two bits set to one.

47
00:04:24,457 --> 00:04:30,766
As a signed number it is minus one, and as an unsigned number it is over four billion.

48
00:04:30,766 --> 00:04:35,002
So slt compares minus one with one, which is true, and gives one.

49
00:04:35,002 --> 00:04:40,606
But sltu compares a number over four billion with one, which is false, and gives zero.

50
00:04:40,606 --> 00:04:46,405
In C the type comes from a variable's declaration, but in assembly the bits have no type,

51
00:04:46,405 --> 00:04:50,933
and the instruction decides whether they are signed or unsigned.

52
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:59,808
The immediate versions compare a register with a constant, and they are called slti, and sltiu.

53
00:04:59,808 --> 00:05:07,033
With x5 unchanged, slti x7 x5 10 gives one, and sltiu gives zero.

54
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:14,363
By the way, multiplication, mul, is not in the RV32I base instruction set, but belongs to the M extension.

55
00:05:14,363 --> 00:05:20,864
General multiplication needs far more circuitry than a shift, so it stays out of the base set.

56
00:05:20,864 --> 00:05:28,400
Division and remainder are in the M extension too, and floating point is in the F extension.

57
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:29,765
Back to branches.

58
00:05:29,765 --> 00:05:36,566
First an if-else, where the variables x, y, z, i, and j live in x10 through x14.

59
00:05:36,566 --> 00:05:38,990
This if-else has two translations.

60
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:44,336
Option B is the same as in episode five, using bne to invert the condition.

61
00:05:44,336 --> 00:05:52,747
Option A doesn't invert, and uses beq to jump to If when the values are equal, so the else part ends up written first.

62
00:05:52,747 --> 00:05:56,666
Both are correct, and both take four instructions.

63
00:05:56,666 --> 00:05:58,337
What if there is no else?

64
00:05:58,337 --> 00:06:05,221
Option A uses beq, jumping to If to run the add when the values are equal, but when they are not equal,

65
00:06:05,221 --> 00:06:08,566
it needs a j End to get around the add.

66
00:06:08,566 --> 00:06:13,283
Option B uses bne, jumping straight to End when the values are not equal,

67
00:06:13,283 --> 00:06:16,708
and running on down into the add when they are equal.

68
00:06:16,708 --> 00:06:23,815
Both are correct, but B follows the structure of the C code and needs only two instructions, one fewer than A,

69
00:06:23,815 --> 00:06:26,666
so it performs better too.

70
00:06:26,666 --> 00:06:30,216
Assembly has no while or for, only branches and jumps.

71
00:06:30,216 --> 00:06:35,213
They work like the goto in C, jumping to a label and carrying on from there.

72
00:06:35,213 --> 00:06:40,473
In real C programs you shouldn't write goto, because it makes code hard to read,

73
00:06:40,473 --> 00:06:47,533
and xkcd even says it summons velociraptors, but it is a very handy intermediate step for translation.

74
00:06:47,533 --> 00:06:55,715
The recipe is to rewrite a control structure into gotos first, and then turn them into instructions one line at a time.

75
00:06:55,715 --> 00:06:59,933
To write real instructions, let cond be x5 less than x6.

76
00:06:59,933 --> 00:07:02,274
That is how the if we just saw works.

77
00:07:02,274 --> 00:07:08,266
It becomes if not cond, goto AfterIf, so when the condition fails, we skip the then part.

78
00:07:08,266 --> 00:07:15,124
A while loop tests at the top and exits when the condition fails, and a goto Loop at the bottom returns to the top,

79
00:07:15,124 --> 00:07:18,966
which is just what the loop in episode five did.

80
00:07:18,966 --> 00:07:24,566
A break is just a goto to the code after the loop, which becomes a single j.

81
00:07:24,566 --> 00:07:27,281
A for loop is first rewritten as a while loop,

82
00:07:27,281 --> 00:07:34,008
with the initialization startline moved in front of the loop and the increment incline put at the end of the body,

83
00:07:34,008 --> 00:07:37,366
and then it is translated just like a while loop.

84
00:07:37,366 --> 00:07:43,432
A do-while runs the body first and tests at the end, jumping back to Loop if the condition holds,

85
00:07:43,432 --> 00:07:47,066
so there is no negation and no j.

86
00:07:47,066 --> 00:07:52,199
Finally, a complete example that adds up the twenty elements of the int array arr.

87
00:07:52,199 --> 00:07:59,066
First we rewrite it as gotos, inverting the condition so that when i is at least twenty, we jump to End.

88
00:07:59,066 --> 00:08:03,203
This is the assembly from the notes, with not a single comment.

89
00:08:03,203 --> 00:08:06,617
What does each register correspond to in the C code?

90
00:08:06,617 --> 00:08:10,228
Match the registers above with the C expressions below.

91
00:08:10,228 --> 00:08:12,789
As a hint, x8 holds the address of arr.

92
00:08:12,789 --> 00:08:18,700
Pause for a moment and try it yourself.

93
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:25,066
The register x8 holds the address of the first element, which is the address of the array arr.

94
00:08:25,066 --> 00:08:32,166
The register x11 is i, which is cleared before the loop and goes up by one at the end of every round.

95
00:08:32,166 --> 00:08:35,209
The register x9 holds the address of arr[i].

96
00:08:35,209 --> 00:08:41,641
It starts from x8 and adds four every round rather than one, because an int takes four bytes,

97
00:08:41,641 --> 00:08:44,166
and that is pointer arithmetic.

98
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:52,866
The register x12 is arr[i], a value rather than an address, which lw reads from the address that x9 points to.

99
00:08:52,866 --> 00:09:01,866
The register x10 is sum, which starts at zero, and the add x10 x10 x12 in the loop is sum plus equals arr[i].

100
00:09:01,866 --> 00:09:05,552
The register x13 is the constant twenty.

101
00:09:05,552 --> 00:09:13,845
Since bge can compare only two registers, we first use addi x13 x0 20, which is li x13 20,

102
00:09:13,845 --> 00:09:16,966
to put twenty into a register.

103
00:09:16,966 --> 00:09:25,966
Note that Loop and End are not instructions, just names for addresses, and Loop is the address of the bge instruction.

104
00:09:25,966 --> 00:09:28,001
Compare this with episode five.

105
00:09:28,001 --> 00:09:32,530
There, every round used slli and add to work out A plus four i again,

106
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:38,700
but here a pointer moves forward four bytes each round, which saves one instruction per round.

107
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:45,395
The notes also include a full version that runs in the Venus simulator, where arr holds one to twenty,

108
00:09:45,395 --> 00:09:49,033
and the program prints two hundred ten at the end.

